WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.193
Picture the helper reading box labels.

2
00:00:05.193 --> 00:00:07.523
Desk lamp, one bulb, no shade: easy.

3
00:00:07.523 --> 00:00:12.442
A bookshelf with a list of books: you need a word about what the list holds.

4
00:00:12.442 --> 00:00:15.225
Some labels use shorthand you must look up.

5
00:00:15.225 --> 00:00:17.167
A few are in another language.

6
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:21.604
Today the helper writes a new kind of label, the class's typed shape.

7
00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:23.399
For the lamp: name, a String.

8
00:00:23.399 --> 00:00:24.266
Bulbs, an Int.

9
00:00:24.266 --> 00:00:29.093
And shade, a String with a question mark, which says: this one may be missing.

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.830
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.984
The type is right there in my file.

12
00:00:34.984 --> 00:00:37.999
Why can't the helper just print it as I wrote it?

13
00:00:37.999 --> 00:00:41.013
Why two objects and a function call for one type?

14
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.118
Two quick attempts come to mind: print the type with toString, or look it up and print the name of the class it names.

15
00:00:49.118 --> 00:00:54.365
The lesson's ShapeDemo tries both, on a crate with tricky types and a small Java class.

16
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:56.240
Which one breaks first?

17
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.427
Pause and guess.

18
00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:03.380
Name only breaks at once.

19
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.465
It drops every question mark and every pair of angle brackets.

20
00:01:07.465 --> 00:01:14.645
So shade becomes a String that can never be null, which the first lamp without a shade will quickly disprove.

21
00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:16.885
And the big map becomes just: Map.

22
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:18.448
toString looks right.

23
00:01:18.448 --> 00:01:21.539
The shade, the list, even the big map: perfect.

24
00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:24.169
And that's exactly what makes it a trap.

25
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:30.541
On the type alias, a second name for a type, it prints: typealias Litres, in square brackets.

26
00:01:30.541 --> 00:01:35.013
On the Java class, it prints String, dot dot, String question mark.

27
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.367
If toString had an honest name, it would be called looksAboutRight.

28
00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:44.280
It's a display form for people reading logs, not a promise about Kotlin syntax.

29
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.120
To print a type right, the helper has to know what each word on the label means.

30
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.596
Say a label says Litres.

31
00:01:51.596 --> 00:01:55.347
Is that a box in this house, or shorthand for something else?

32
00:01:55.347 --> 00:01:57.131
The helper has to look it up.

33
00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.801
That's the two objects.

34
00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:03.672
A KSTypeReference is the word as written on the label, not looked up yet.

35
00:02:03.672 --> 00:02:05.674
Calling resolve is the lookup.

36
00:02:05.674 --> 00:02:13.080
The KSType it returns is what the word means: the class it names, whether it may be null, and the types inside.

37
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:18.337
Looking up is real work, through imports, packages and type aliases.

38
00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:23.631
KSP's docs call it one of the most expensive operations in the KSP API.

39
00:02:23.631 --> 00:02:29.372
So KSP resolves nothing until you ask, and you should ask once per reference.

40
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:31.667
The picture breaks a little here.

41
00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:34.125
A person looks a word up and moves on.

42
00:02:34.125 --> 00:02:41.756
KSP writes down every lookup a processor makes, because a later build needs to know which boxes each card depended on.

43
00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:45.590
So we build the text ourselves, in three steps.

44
00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.891
First the name, from the declaration.

45
00:02:47.891 --> 00:02:53.363
Then the type arguments, the types inside the angle brackets, each printed the same way.

46
00:02:53.363 --> 00:02:56.411
Then the mark: a question mark if it may be null.

47
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.332
Each printed the same way.

48
00:02:58.332 --> 00:03:05.061
A list of nullable Ints holds a type that needs exactly the same treatment, so typeText calls itself.

49
00:03:05.933 --> 00:03:12.766
Let's walk the biggest one: a map from String to a list of nullable Ints, where the map itself may be null.

50
00:03:12.766 --> 00:03:14.809
How many times will resolve run?

51
00:03:14.809 --> 00:03:15.831
Pause and guess.

52
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:20.988
Level one, the map.

53
00:03:20.988 --> 00:03:22.101
Its name is Map.

54
00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:26.343
It has two type arguments, and its nullability says nullable.

55
00:03:26.533 --> 00:03:28.227
Level two, the arguments.

56
00:03:28.227 --> 00:03:32.088
String has no arguments and no mark, so it's just String.

57
00:03:32.088 --> 00:03:35.001
List has one argument, so we go down again.

58
00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.388
Level three: Int, marked nullable, so Int with a question mark.

59
00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:41.422
Now the text climbs back up.

60
00:03:41.422 --> 00:03:43.176
List of Int question mark.

61
00:03:43.176 --> 00:03:45.402
Then Map of String and that list.

62
00:03:45.402 --> 00:03:47.965
And last, the map's own question mark.

63
00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:51.192
Four lookups: the map, String, the list, and Int.

64
00:03:51.192 --> 00:03:53.065
One for each type in the tree.

65
00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:56.623
And the result matches toString, character for character.

66
00:03:57.533 --> 00:03:59.560
Two cases are left for the lab.

67
00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:04.333
A star, as in a list of anything, has no type inside, so we print a star.

68
00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:11.591
And a type from Java is a platform type: Kotlin can't tell if it may be null, so it writes an exclamation mark.

69
00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:14.800
In the picture, that's a label in another language.

70
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.303
The helper doesn't guess.

71
00:04:16.303 --> 00:04:20.031
It writes an exclamation mark, the label's version of a shrug.

72
00:04:20.933 --> 00:04:30.540
Put it together, and our column is right on every row: the question marks, the brackets, Litres, the star, and the Java String with its exclamation mark.

73
00:04:31.467 --> 00:04:39.853
A javac annotation processor, the closest Java equivalent, prints a TypeMirror much the same way: the name, then each argument.

74
00:04:39.853 --> 00:04:45.731
But it can't print a question mark at all, because Java's type system has no nullability.

75
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:47.619
So, three rules.

76
00:04:47.619 --> 00:04:51.123
Resolve each reference once, and build from the result.

77
00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:54.372
Keep toString for your eyes, never for your output.

78
00:04:54.372 --> 00:04:59.277
And never print the name alone: it throws away exactly the parts that matter.

79
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.459
So, why two objects for one type?

80
00:05:02.459 --> 00:05:05.129
The reference is the word on the label.

81
00:05:05.129 --> 00:05:07.525
The resolved type is what it means.

82
00:05:07.525 --> 00:05:11.427
Resolve once, then build the text: name, arguments, mark.

83
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:12.392
Quick check.

84
00:05:12.392 --> 00:05:16.816
The crate gets one more parameter, spare, a Crate that may be null.

85
00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:19.919
What do toString, name only and typeText print?

86
00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:26.588
Crate question mark, Crate, and Crate question mark.

87
00:05:26.588 --> 00:05:29.312
Only the name-only column loses the mark.
